(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-11-04"] = {"6.1":{"s":"Oxidation and reduction always happen together, so the reactions are called redox reactions. In terms of electrons, oxidation is the loss of electrons and reduction is the gain of electrons. The substance that loses electrons is the reducing agent, because it reduces the other. The substance that gains electrons is the oxidising agent. Older definitions used the gain or loss of oxygen or hydrogen.","w":["OIL RIG: Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain of electrons.","The oxidising agent is the one that gets reduced; the reducing agent is the one that gets oxidised."],"x":["Oxidation and reduction always happen together in one reaction, so such a reaction is called a redox reaction. In terms of electrons, oxidation is the loss of electrons, and reduction is the gain of electrons. A good memory aid is OIL RIG: Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain.","The substance that loses electrons is oxidised, and it makes the other substance gain electrons, so it is the reducing agent. The substance that gains electrons is reduced, and it is the oxidising agent. So the oxidising agent is the one that is reduced, and the reducing agent is the one that is oxidised.","The older definitions are still useful. Oxidation is the addition of oxygen or the removal of hydrogen, and reduction is the removal of oxygen or the addition of hydrogen. For example, when magnesium burns in oxygen, magnesium gains oxygen and is oxidised. The electron definition is the most general, and works for reactions with no oxygen or hydrogen at all.","Common mistake: mixing up the agent with the change. The reducing agent is the substance that is itself oxidised."],"e":"In Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu, zinc loses two electrons, so it is oxidised and acts as the reducing agent. Cu²⁺ gains two electrons, so it is reduced and acts as the oxidising agent."},"6.2":{"s":"The oxidation number is the charge an atom would have if all its bonds were treated as ionic. It follows simple rules: it is zero for an atom in an element, equal to the charge for a simple ion, +1 for hydrogen and −2 for oxygen in most compounds, and the numbers in a neutral compound add up to zero. A rise in oxidation number is oxidation, and a fall is reduction.","f":[{"x":"Σ oxidation numbers = charge on the species (0 for a neutral molecule)","n":"Sum of oxidation numbers. O is −1 in peroxides; H is −1 in metal hydrides."}],"w":["Oxygen is −2 except in peroxides (−1, as in H₂O₂) and in OF₂; hydrogen is −1 in metal hydrides.","In an ion the oxidation numbers add up to the charge of the ion."],"x":["The oxidation number (oxidation state) of an atom is the charge it would have if all the bonds in the compound were treated as ionic, with the shared electrons given to the more electronegative atom.","Rules. An atom in a free element, such as Na, O₂ or S₈, has an oxidation number of zero. A simple ion has an oxidation number equal to its charge (Na⁺ is +1, Cl⁻ is −1). In compounds, hydrogen is +1 (but −1 in metal hydrides like NaH), and oxygen is −2 (but −1 in peroxides such as H₂O₂, and +2 in OF₂). Fluorine is always −1. The sum of the oxidation numbers is zero in a neutral compound, and equals the charge in an ion.","To find an unknown number, call it x and write the sum. In KMnO₄: (+1) + x + 4(−2) = 0, so x = +7. In Cr₂O₇²⁻: 2x + 7(−2) = −2, so x = +6. In Na₂S₂O₃ the average for sulfur is +2.","A rise in oxidation number means oxidation, and a fall means reduction. This lets us find the oxidising and reducing agents even in complicated reactions. Common mistake: forgetting the number of atoms of the element in the formula, such as the 2 in Cr₂O₇²⁻."],"e":"In 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, hydrogen goes from 0 to +1 (oxidised), and oxygen from 0 to −2 (reduced). So H₂ is the reducing agent, and O₂ is the oxidising agent."},"6.3":{"s":"A redox equation can be balanced by two methods. In the oxidation number method, find the atoms whose oxidation number changes, balance the total increase and decrease, and then balance the other atoms. In the half-reaction (ion-electron) method, split the reaction into an oxidation half and a reduction half, balance atoms and charges in each, equalise the electrons lost and gained, and add the halves.","f":[{"x":"MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O","n":"Permanganate half-reaction"},{"x":"Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O","n":"Dichromate half-reaction. n-factor is 6 in acidic medium."}],"w":["In acid solution balance oxygen with H₂O and then hydrogen with H⁺; in basic solution add OH⁻ at the end.","Electrons lost in the oxidation half must equal the electrons gained in the reduction half before adding."],"x":["Redox equations are often hard to balance by trial, so two systematic methods are used. Both need the electrons lost and gained to be equal.","Oxidation number method: (1) write the skeleton equation; (2) find the atoms whose oxidation numbers change, and find the total change for each; (3) multiply the species so that the total increase equals the total decrease; (4) balance the other atoms, first the ones other than H and O, then O with water and H with H⁺ (in acid) or OH⁻ (in base); (5) check the atoms and the charge.","Half-reaction (ion-electron) method: (1) split the reaction into an oxidation half and a reduction half; (2) balance each half for atoms other than H and O; (3) balance O by adding H₂O, and H by adding H⁺; (4) balance the charge by adding electrons; (5) multiply the halves so the electrons are equal, and add them. In basic solution, add OH⁻ to both sides to turn each H⁺ into water.","Always check at the end that the numbers of atoms of each element, and the total charge, are the same on both sides. Common mistake: adding H⁺ in a basic solution, or forgetting to equalise the electrons."],"e":"Acid solution: MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O, and Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻. Multiply the second by 5 and add: MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O + 5Fe³⁺. Check the charge: left −1 + 8 + 10 = +17, and right +2 + 15 = +17."},"6.4":{"s":"In a Daniell cell, zinc loses electrons at the anode and copper ions gain electrons at the cathode, and the electrons flow through the outer circuit. The potential of an electrode is its tendency to be reduced. It is measured against the standard hydrogen electrode, which is given 0.00 V. A more positive E° means a stronger oxidising agent, and a more negative E° means a stronger reducing agent.","f":[{"x":"E° = E°(reduction half) + E°(oxidation half)","n":"Reverse the sign of the table value for the half that is oxidised, then add. A positive sum means the reaction is spontaneous.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 6.4, p.90"}],"w":["Table values are for reduction. For the half that is oxidised, change the sign before adding.","A large negative E° means a strong reducing agent (lithium, potassium); a large positive E° a strong oxidising agent (fluorine)."],"x":["A redox reaction can produce electricity if the two halves are kept in separate containers, joined by a wire and a salt bridge. In the Daniell cell, a zinc rod in zinc sulfate solution is joined to a copper rod in copper sulfate solution. Zinc loses electrons (it is the anode, where oxidation happens), the electrons flow through the wire to the copper rod, and copper ions gain them (the cathode, where reduction happens). The salt bridge completes the circuit.","The electrode potential measures the tendency of an electrode to gain electrons, that is, to be reduced. It is measured against the standard hydrogen electrode, which is defined as 0.00 V. Standard electrode potentials (E°) are quoted for reduction, at 1 M concentration, 1 bar and 298 K. A more positive E° means a stronger oxidising agent, and a more negative E° means a stronger reducing agent.","The cell potential is E°cell = E°(cathode) − E°(anode), using the reduction values for both. For the Daniell cell, E°cell = 0.34 − (−0.76) = +1.10 V. A positive E°cell means that the reaction is spontaneous in the direction written.","Common mistakes: changing the sign of the E° value for the cathode, and not changing the direction for the half that is oxidised. Remember that E° values do not change when you multiply the half-reaction by a number."],"e":"Will copper displace zinc from zinc sulfate solution? Using Cu²⁺/Cu = +0.34 V and Zn²⁺/Zn = −0.76 V, the reaction Cu + Zn²⁺ → Cu²⁺ + Zn has E° = −0.76 − 0.34 = −1.10 V. It is negative, so it does not happen."}};
